// The other side of `bare_value_coerces_one_layer.zm`: a bare value is wrapped one layer, so where
// two are needed and no single-layer reading exists, it is refused rather than guessed at. Here the
// function answers `Result<Option<Found>, string>` and a bare `Found` matches neither `Ok(Found)`
// nor `Err(string)` -- the message names what was returned and what was declared, and `ok(Some(f))`
// is what to write.
//
// Worth pinning beside the other one because the *same* shape with a `string` payload does compile,
// as `Err`: there the single-layer reading exists and is taken. The refusal is what makes that
// behaviour a rule rather than an accident.
struct Found {
    at: int
}

fn lookup(n: int) -> Result<Option<Found>, string> {
    return Found { at: n }
}

fn main() {
    print(str(unwrap_Some(unwrap(lookup(3))).at))
}
